			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2265278">
									

<p>The idea of something that allows you to see horrible things that go unnoticed by the rest of humanity, on its own, has been done to the point where I believe something else of interest beyond &quot;well I guess that's a thing&quot; is necessary before it's a workable article. The fact that it's unnoticeable surgery is the start of something interesting, I suppose.</p>
<p>I really didn't care for the &quot;and it was a spooooky cult&quot; explanation, which isn't elucidated at all beyond the fact that it exists and that it made this stuff. I'm not saying that I want to read reams of reports on a goofy cult, because I don't think I do, but the offhanded nature of the reference makes it feel like a &quot;eh, a wizard did it or something&quot; handwaved explanation.</p>
<p>In some ways, I think no explanation at all would be preferable. The fact that any old goober can put some household chemicals together and suddenly be aware of this bizarre phenomenon is the basis of something interesting; making it the product of the Foundationverse's apparently-thriving Spooky Doomsday Cult Community only cheapens the horror, I feel.</p>

							</div>

								
					
